      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Mind-body interventions can improve vulnerabilities that underlie smoking behavior. The characteristics of smokers who use mind-body medicine have not been explored, preventing the development of targeted interventions. Patients ( N\u2009=\u2009593) presenting to a mind-body medicine clinic completed self-report measures. Patients were 67\u2009percent never smokers, 27\u2009percent former smokers, and 6\u2009percent current smokers. Current smokers were younger; more likely to be single, unemployed, or on disability; and report greater depression symptoms, greater pain, and lower social support ( ps\u2009<\u2009.05).Current smokers who use mind-body medicine have unique psychosocial needs that should be targeted in mind-body smoking cessation interventions."},
